    {
      "id": 4,
      "slug": "static-to-dynamic-ai-cluster-reconfiguration",
      "title": "Static-to-Dynamic AI Cluster Reconfiguration",
      "description_html": "",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "type": "image",
          "variant": "full",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "LIGHT",
            "card_mode": "WHITE",
            "image_opacity": 0.5,
            "image_opacity_color": "#000000",
            "zoom_on_click": false,
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 0,
            "padding_bottom": 0,
            "custom_padding_top": 0,
            "custom_padding_bottom": 0,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO",
            "entrance_animation": true
          },
          "image_role": "decorative",
          "media": {
            "path": "resources/images/why-4.png",
            "alt": "Why_4.png",
            "source_asset": "Why_4.png",
            "mime_type": "image/png",
            "bytes": 9256,
            "dimensions": {
              "originalWidth": 1680,
              "originalHeight": 222
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "type": "text",
          "variant": "paragraph",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "COLOR",
            "background_color": "#f1f1f1",
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 3,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO"
          },
          "body_html": "<p>We explored the foundational concepts of <strong>Optical Circuit Switching (OCS)</strong> and its significance in modern <strong>AI infrastructure</strong>.</p><p>Let's now dive into a practical application of these principles. We will examine how <strong>Google's TPUv4 supercomputer</strong> leverages <strong>OCS</strong> to create dynamic and scalable <strong>AI clusters</strong>. Click on each marker to learn more.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "type": "labeled_graphic",
          "variant": "labeledgraphic",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "COLOR",
            "background_color": "#f2f2f1",
            "zoom_on_click": true,
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 3,
            "media_width": "1",
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO",
            "media_width_variant": "medium"
          },
          "items": [
            {
              "title": "Inside Google's TPUv4",
              "x": "36.95188613164993",
              "y": "47.54409386367394",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<p>Google's TPUv4 supercomputer utilizes OCS to build physically reconfigurable AI clusters. The system is built on a base unit called a Cube, which houses 64 TPUs hardwired together in a four-by-four-by-four electrical mesh. All connections within a single Cube are electrical.</p>"
            },
            {
              "title": "The Superpod Scaling",
              "x": "75.38070678710938",
              "y": "37.55952380952381",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<p>To scale outward, 64 Cubes are networked together to form a Superpod containing 4,096 TPUs. These Cubes are interconnected using 48 MEMS-based 128-port optical circuit switches. The OCS fabric is divided into three distinct groups handling the X, Y, and Z dimensions of the network.</p>"
            },
            {
              "title": "Dynamic Reconfiguration of Optical Switches",
              "x": "58.392857142857146",
              "y": "76.08245365203373",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<p>A software control plane dynamically reconfigures these optical switches on a per-job basis. This allows the network topology to physically shift, creating customized pathways like a 3D torus that perfectly match the communication demands of the active workload.</p>"
            }
          ],
          "media": {
            "path": "resources/images/3.jpg",
            "alt": "3.jpg",
            "source_asset": "3.jpg",
            "mime_type": "image/jpeg",
            "bytes": 91625,
            "dimensions": {
              "originalWidth": 1680,
              "originalHeight": 945
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "type": "text",
          "variant": "subheading",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "COLOR",
            "background_color": "#f2f2f1",
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 0,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO"
          },
          "heading_html": "<p>As AI systems scale, hardware failures become routine. Switches, links, and optical modules will <span style=\"color: #8cc53f; text-decoration: inherit\" data-color-scope=\"inner\">inevitably fail</span>. </p>"
