        {
          "id": 2,
          "type": "text",
          "variant": "paragraph",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "COLOR",
            "background_color": "#1c1c1c",
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 3,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO"
          },
          "body_html": "<p><strong>Traditional network recovery</strong> typically relies on <strong>software-based rerouting</strong>, which can degrade <strong>bandwidth</strong> by forcing data through remaining pathways. In contrast, <strong>Optical Circuit Switching (OCS)</strong> provides <strong>programmable physical-layer resiliency</strong>. This allows operators to maintain a shared pool of <strong>spare optical links</strong> rather than over-provisioning active paths.</p><p>Below, you can watch  how a <strong>control plane</strong> detects a <strong>spine switch failure</strong> and instructs the <strong>OCS fabric</strong> to restore full bandwidth.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "type": "video",
          "variant": "video",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "COLOR",
            "background_color": "#1c1c1c",
