    {
      "id": 6,
      "slug": "knowledge-check",
      "title": "Knowledge Check",
      "description_html": "<p>Before moving on to the next module, test your knowledge of these production deployment models.</p><p></p><p>Select \"Start Quiz\" to begin.</p>",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>In Google's Jupiter network, what is the primary reason OCS spine replacement does not constrain future leaf and interconnect upgrades?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It utilizes deep packet buffers</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It relies heavily on electrical signal regeneration</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It operates as a rate-agnostic optical layer</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It uses liquid crystal polarization</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. Because OCS is protocol and rate agnostic, operators can upgrade leaf switches to higher speeds without having to replace the optical spine.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. The optical spine does not impose speed constraints because it is a rate-agnostic physical layer.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>In the TPUv4 Superpod architecture, OCS connectivity exists between which structural units?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Between Cubes</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Between individual TPUs within a server</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Between CPU host processors</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Between memory modules</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. Interconnections within a 64-TPU Cube are purely electrical, while OCS provides dynamic connectivity between the Cubes.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. OCS provides connectivity between the Cubes, whereas internal Cube connections are entirely electrical.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>A full TPUv4 Superpod utilizes how many OCS devices in total?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>16</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>32</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>48</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>64</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. A Superpod contains 48 MEMS optical circuit switches, divided into three groups of 16 for the X, Y, and Z dimensions.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. The Superpod utilizes 48 total OCS devices to connect 64 Cubes.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>What distinguishes OCS-based failure recovery from traditional rerouting in terms of bandwidth restoration?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>OCS routes traffic through slower backup copper links</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>OCS relies on packet buffering to absorb lost data</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>OCS forces training jobs to rollback to previous checkpoints</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>OCS physically restores full intended bandwidth by steering light to spare hardware</p>",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. Instead of running in a degraded bandwidth state, OCS reconfigures physical optical paths to spare resources to fully restore original performance.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. OCS restores full intended bandwidth by physically reconnecting pathways to spare components.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
